## How to answer in the interview

> "Fiber is React's reconciler, rewritten in React 16. The old one was a synchronous recursive walk on the call stack — once it started it couldn't be interrupted, so a big update would block the main thread and cause jank.
>
> Fiber reimplements that work as a linked tree of plain objects, each one a unit of work with child, sibling, and parent pointers. Because it's data rather than stack frames, React can render part of the tree, yield to the browser, and resume — which is what makes prioritization and everything in Concurrent React possible.
>
> And React doesn't diff the DOM directly. It diffs fiber trees, and flushes the resulting DOM operations in one commit."

## Key takeaways

- Elements are cheap, throwaway descriptions recreated on every render
- The fiber tree is the persistent structure React actually walks and diffs
- A fiber is **just an object** — a unit of work with `child` / `sibling` / `return` pointers
- Replacing the call stack with a data structure is what bought interruptibility
- Only the output of the diff ever touches the real DOM

Think of a movie production. The director shoots and edits behind closed doors, trying takes and discarding them — the audience sees none of it. Then the finished cut is projected onto the screen in one go. **Render** is the editing room; **commit** is the projection.

React Fiber splits its work into these two phases for exactly one reason: so the editing can be interrupted without the audience ever seeing a half-finished film.

## 1. The Render phase — planning

When state or props change, React enters the render phase. Here it is **only calculating** what should change. Nothing reaches the screen.

**What happens:** React calls your component functions to work out what the UI should look like, builds a work-in-progress tree of fibers, and diffs it against the current tree — reconciliation ([/react-guide/4](/react-guide/4)). The output is a list of the precise changes needed.

**It is interruptible.** All of this happens in memory, so React can pause it, let the browser handle a click or paint a frame, and then resume — or abort and restart entirely if the work became obsolete.

**Therefore it must be pure.** This is the rule that follows directly from interruptibility: because React may pause, abort, restart, or double-invoke a render, your component body must not have side effects. No DOM mutation, no mutating external variables, no network requests in the component body.

Strict Mode's deliberate double-invocation in development exists to surface exactly these impurities ([/react-guide/33](/react-guide/33)).

## 2. The Commit phase — execution

Once render has produced the change list, React applies it.

**What happens:** React mutates the real DOM — `appendChild`, `removeChild`, attribute updates — then runs your effects.

**It is synchronous and uninterruptible.** Once React starts mutating the DOM it cannot stop partway. If it did, the user would see a genuinely broken, half-updated UI. Commit is **atomic**: either every DOM mutation for that render happens, or none does.

**This is where side effects live.** After the DOM is updated, refs are attached and your effect hooks fire — which is why it's safe to measure or touch DOM nodes there.

## The full sequence

```javascript
Render (call components, build WIP tree)
  → Reconciliation (diff against last committed tree)
  → Commit (mutate the real DOM)
  → useLayoutEffect (sync, blocks paint)
  → Browser paint
  → useEffect (async, after paint)
```

That single ordering resolves more "which hook runs when" questions than anything else in React.

**`useLayoutEffect` runs after the DOM changes but before the browser paints** — so if you measure an element and reposition it there, the user never sees the intermediate state. **`useEffect` runs after paint**, so doing the same work there produces a visible flicker as the element jumps ([/react-guide/8](/react-guide/8)).

The cost is symmetrical: `useLayoutEffect` blocks painting, so slow work in it delays what the user sees.

