---
## Summary comparison

| Feature | Render phase | Commit phase |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Purpose** | Calculate changes (reconciliation) | Apply changes to the DOM |
| **Execution** | Asynchronous / **interruptible** | Synchronous / **uninterruptible** |
| **Purity** | Must be pure — no side effects | Side effects allowed and expected |
| **User visibility** | Invisible — happens in memory | Visible — updates the screen |
| **Can be thrown away** | Yes | No — atomic once started |
| **Your code that runs here** | Component bodies, `useMemo`, `useState` updaters | `useLayoutEffect`, `useEffect`, refs, cleanup |

## How to answer in the interview

> "React splits an update into render and commit. Render calls your components and diffs the fiber trees to work out what changed — it's in-memory only, so React can pause, resume, or throw it away. That's why component bodies have to be pure: React might run them more than once or discard the result.
>
> Commit is where the calculated changes hit the real DOM. It's synchronous and can't be interrupted, because a half-applied DOM would be visibly broken. Effects run at the end of commit — `useLayoutEffect` before paint, `useEffect` after."

## Key takeaways

- Nothing in render may have a side effect — React can call it more than once
- Interruptibility is *why* purity is required; the two are the same fact
- Commit is atomic: all the DOM mutations land, or none do
- `useLayoutEffect` before paint, `useEffect` after — that's the flicker/blocking trade-off

A general tree-diff algorithm is `O(n³)`. React makes it `O(n)` with two assumptions that are true almost all the time in UI trees:

| Comparison | What React does |
| --- | --- |
| Different element **type** at a position | Tear down the whole old subtree (unmount), build the new one fresh |
| Same element **type** | Keep the Fiber & DOM node, patch only the changed props |
| List children | Match by `key`, not position — a stable key says "this is the same logical item," even if it moved |

That's the whole reason `key` matters: without it, React falls back to matching by array index, and reordering, inserting, or removing at the front of a list makes it misattribute state to the wrong item — see the Gotcha Bank entry on `key={index}`.

## Key takeaways

- Different type at a position = full tear-down and rebuild of that subtree.
- Same type = props patched in place, DOM node reused.
- `key` gives list items stable identity across renders — it's not just for silencing a warning.

## 📊 How It Works

![Reconciliation diffs by position, then by type](/diagrams/react/reconciliation_keys.svg)

