## How to answer in the interview

> "Props flow down, events flow up. Anything that looks like two-way binding in React — a controlled input — is really just this loop running on every keystroke."

`useEffect` lets a component reach outside React to synchronize with something the render itself can't touch — the DOM, a browser API, a subscription, a network request, a timer. You hand it a function; React runs that function after the DOM has been committed and the browser has painted (see [The Render → Commit → Effects Pipeline](/react-guide/3)).

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  // runs after paint, whenever a listed dependency changed
  const subscription = api.subscribe(handleUpdate);

  return () => {
    // cleanup — runs before the next effect, and on unmount
    subscription.unsubscribe();
  };
}, [api]); // dependency array
```

## The three dependency-array shapes

| Array | Runs |
| --- | --- |
| Omitted | After **every** render — rarely what you want |
| `[]` | Once, after the initial mount |
| `[a, b]` | After mount, then again whenever `a` or `b` changes by reference |

## 📊 How It Works

![Effect dependencies and cleanup order](/diagrams/react/effect_deps_cleanup.svg)

