## How to answer in the interview

> "`useEffect` synchronizes a component with something outside React — it's not a lifecycle hook standing in for `componentDidMount`, it's a hook for keeping an external system in sync with the latest render's values."

## The rest of the hook roster

One line each — the deep dives cover the traps and internals.

| Hook | What it's for |
| --- | --- |
| `useState` | Local state that, when set, schedules a re-render |
| `useEffect` | Synchronize with something outside React, after paint |
| `useLayoutEffect` | Same as `useEffect`, but synchronous and before paint — for layout reads/writes |
| `useRef` | A mutable box that survives renders without ever causing one |
| `useReducer` | `useState` with the transition logic centralized in one pure reducer function |
| `useContext` | Read the nearest Provider's value; re-renders on every value change |
| `useMemo` | Memoize a computed **value** between renders |
| `useCallback` | Memoize a **function reference** between renders |
| `useTransition` | Mark a state update as non-urgent/interruptible |
| `useDeferredValue` | Defer using a received value until more urgent work finishes |
| `useId` | Generate a stable, SSR-safe unique id |
| `use` | Unwrap a promise or Context — the only hook callable conditionally |

## Going deeper

Each of these gets its own deep dive elsewhere in this guide: [useState internals](/react-guide/6), [useEffect vs useLayoutEffect](/react-guide/8), [useMemo & useCallback](/react-guide/9), [useRef](/react-guide/10), [useReducer vs useState](/react-guide/11), [useTransition vs useDeferredValue](/react-guide/32).

Conceptually, React keeps a per-Fiber array of hook slots and an index that resets to `0` at the start of every render. Each call to `useState` reads and advances that index; the setter it returns is a **closure** that remembers which slot it owns.

```javascript
const React = (function () {
  let memory = [];
  let index = 0;

  return {
    render(Component) {
      index = 0; // reset before every render
      return Component();
    },
    useState(initialValue) {
      const slot = index;
      if (memory[slot] === undefined) memory[slot] = initialValue;

      const setState = (next) => {
        memory[slot] = typeof next === 'function' ? next(memory[slot]) : next;
      };

      index++;
      return [memory[slot], setState];
    },
  };
})();
```

This is **why** hook order must never change between renders — slot `0` has to mean the same piece of state every time, or the array shifts and every hook after the shift reads the wrong slot.

