`useMemo(calculate, deps)` runs `calculate` during render and caches the result. On later renders, if every dependency is still `Object.is`-equal to last time, React skips re-running `calculate` and hands back the cached value instead.

```javascript
function ProductList({ products, query }) {
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => products.filter((p) => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())),
    [products, query]
  );
  return <List items={filtered} />;
}
```

`calculate` runs **during render**, so it must stay pure — no state updates, no subscriptions, no fetches inside it. That's the line that separates it from `useEffect`.

## Gotchas

- **It's a hint, not a guarantee.** React may discard a memoized value to reclaim memory (e.g. offscreen components) and recompute it on the next render anyway — never rely on `useMemo` for correctness, only performance.
- **The dependency array itself needs stable references.** `useMemo(() => x, [{ a, b }])` recomputes every render, because the object literal in the array is new every time — memoize or destructure the primitives instead.
- **Cheap computations lose money.** The dependency comparison and cache storage aren't free; wrapping `a + b` costs more than it saves.

