## Tricky interview questions

**Q: Why is `use()` allowed after an early `return`, when `useState` would break the rules of hooks in the same spot?**
A: `useState`'s slot is identified purely by call order — a conditional call shifts every later hook's index. `use()` doesn't rely on a fixed slot the same way; it's designed specifically to be called conditionally.

**Q: A component calls `use(fetch('/api/user'))` directly in its body. What goes wrong?**
A: `fetch(...)` runs fresh every render, producing a new promise each time — the component suspends again on every render instead of resolving once, effectively an infinite loading state.

**Q: What happens when the promise passed to `use()` rejects?**
A: It throws to the nearest error boundary, the same way a thrown error during render does — not to a `.catch()` you write locally.

## How to answer in the interview

> "`use()` unwraps a promise or Context, and it's allowed to be conditional because it doesn't depend on hook call order the way `useState` does. The catch is the promise has to be stable — created outside the component, not freshly on every render — or it suspends forever."

A custom hook is only worth being a hook if it calls other hooks. If a piece of logic is pure computation — formatting, sorting, validation — it should be a plain function, not a `useSomething`. Mixing the two either breaks the rules of hooks, or couples code to React for no reason and makes it harder to unit test.

```javascript
// Pure helper — no hooks, trivially testable
function generateRandomTasks(count) { /* ...pure computation... */ }

// Custom hook — owns state + effects, composes other hooks
function useTasks(initialCount = 5) {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState(() => generateRandomTasks(initialCount));
  const addTask = useCallback((title) => {
    setTasks(prev => [...prev, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), title, done: false }]);
  }, []);
  return { tasks, addTask };
}
```

