## Gotcha

`<input defaultValue="hello" />` is **uncontrolled** — the DOM node owns subsequent changes even though it had an initial value from React. Switching an input from uncontrolled to controlled (or the reverse) mid-lifecycle throws a React warning.

Refs don't pass through function components by default — `forwardRef` lets a parent reach a child's underlying DOM node or a curated imperative API.

```javascript
const FancyInput = React.forwardRef(function FancyInput(props, ref) {
  const innerRef = useRef(null);
  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus: () => innerRef.current?.focus(),
    clear: () => { innerRef.current.value = ''; },
  }));
  return <input ref={innerRef} {...props} />;
});
```

`useImperativeHandle` exposes a limited, intentional API instead of the raw node — use it for design-system primitives that parents must occasionally control imperatively (focus management, modals, form libraries).

