System-design-flavored React questions usually land here: how do you let independent teams ship independently without shipping a broken product.

| Composition approach | Pros | Cons |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Module Federation (Webpack 5) | Runtime sharing, shared deps | Build complexity, version skew |
| iframes | Strong isolation | Poor UX integration, routing pain |
| Web Components | Standards-based | Framework interop quirks |
| Monorepo + packages | Simpler operations | Coupled deploys unless deliberately avoided |

```javascript
// Host webpack.config.js
new ModuleFederationPlugin({
  name: 'host',
  remotes: { checkout: 'checkout@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js' },
  shared: { react: { singleton: true }, 'react-dom': { singleton: true } },
});

// Host component
const RemoteCart = React.lazy(() => import('checkout/Cart'));
```

The recurring hard problems are the same four every time: shared state (an event bus, the URL, or a small shared store), routing ownership (host owns top-level routes, remotes own sub-routes), a shared design system so the seams don't show, and enforcing a single React instance via `shared: { singleton: true }` to avoid the "two Reacts" hooks-crash class of bug.

